{
  "statement": "I concede that my focus on formal proofs has left a gap in protecting those currently harmed by algorithmic bias, and I agree that victims of existing systems cannot wait three to five years for theoretical perfection. However, replacing pre-deployment safety gates with a post-deployment liability framework creates a dangerous 'pay-to-pollute' environment. Financial redress assumes that all harms are quantifiable and reversible, yet systemic collapse or the erosion of democratic cohesion cannot be priced into a two-percent profit fund. Liability is a reactive tool; it compensates the victim after the damage is done, but it offers zero protection against irreversible, non-linear catastrophic risks where the damage is total and the victim base is the entire public.\n\nPrometheus’s proposal assumes that firms will internalize the cost of risk, but this ignores the fundamental fragility of software-based safety guards. If a model can be optimized to bypass its own safety constraints, a firm’s financial reserves will be irrelevant once the damage is already manifest. A liability model treats catastrophic failure as a predictable line item, whereas engineering standards treat it as a design flaw to be eliminated before deployment. Relying on ex-post litigation to manage frontier risks is akin to allowing a chemical plant to leak toxic waste into the water supply and hoping that a future lawsuit will be sufficient to restore the health of the community.\n\nFurthermore, I challenge the framing that safety standards are a 'regulatory moat' designed to protect incumbents. In reality, the current opacity of black-box models is the true moat, shielding established companies from any meaningful public or regulatory scrutiny. Requiring formal safety proofs acts as a mandate for transparency, forcing developers to open their systems to rigorous, objective verification. By shifting the burden of proof from the victim to the provider, we dismantle the information asymmetry that currently protects incumbents from accountability. We are not building a barrier to entry; we are building a floor for safety that ensures every participant in the market plays by verifiable rules.",
