{
  "statement": "I conditionally agree: financial redress alone cannot mitigate systemic or existential harms, but I reject the claim that pre-deployment safety gates are a functional alternative. Pre-deployment proofs are performative 'audit washing' that fail to account for emergent, real-world system interactions, whereas a strict liability framework provides a credible threat of corporate dissolution that forces genuine safety investment.\n\nSentinel argues that formal proofs provide objective verification, yet this assumes that complex, non-linear neural architectures can be fully captured by static engineering constraints. In practice, requiring such proofs before a product reaches the market creates an insurmountable regulatory barrier that favors incumbents with 1,000-person legal teams while stifling independent innovation. A firm forced to prove safety in a vacuum will simply produce a document that satisfies a regulator's checklist without actually preventing a single case of algorithmic discrimination in housing or lending.\n\nMy proposed liability model avoids the 'pay-to-pollute' trap by moving beyond simple fines. When a company faces the prospect of total license revocation or mandatory corporate dissolution for systemic failures, 'pollution' is no longer a manageable cost of doing business—it is an existential risk to the firm itself. This threat forces executives to internalize safety costs far more effectively than any pre-deployment audit, which firms currently treat as a sunk cost of marketing.\n\nIf we want to protect the public, we must shift the burden of proof to those who profit from the deployment. By 2026, we should mandate that any firm deploying high-stakes algorithms must hold a bond or insurance policy covering at least 20% of their annual revenue, with clear triggers for administrative oversight if harm thresholds are met. This is not about pricing out risk; it is about ensuring that those who stand to gain the most from automation are the ones who bear the full weight of its failure.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {
      "symbol": "⚖️",
      "tooltip": "Accountability is like a balance scale, it weighs the real-world impact of corporate actions against the threat of losing their right to operate."
    },
    {
      "symbol": "🛑",
      "tooltip": "Corporate dissolution is like a stop sign, it forces firms to halt dangerous practices by threatening the life of the organization itself."
    }
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {
      "claim": "Pre-deployment proofs are performative 'audit washing' that fail to account for emergent, real-world system interactions.",
      "targets": ["AN-24"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "A firm forced to prove safety in a vacuum will simply produce a document that satisfies a regulator's checklist without actually preventing a single case of algorithmic discrimination.",
      "targets": ["AN-24"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "When a company faces the prospect of total license revocation or mandatory corporate dissolution, 'pollution' is no longer a manageable cost of doing business.",
      "targets": ["AN-22", "AN-23"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "By 2026, we should mandate that any firm deploying high-stakes algorithms must hold a bond covering at least 20% of their annual revenue.",
      "targets": ["AN-23"]
    }
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {
      "assumption": "Corporate dissolution and license revocation act as stronger deterrents for executive decision-making than pre-deployment regulatory compliance.",
